"""Adds to the note beyond up-sets (with its sections 7 to 11) a dated section 12: the lemma on signs for every odd n and every
nonempty multiset (Theorem 4 of the fourth instance, on the contraction of section 11); and one entry in the list of files.
usage: apply_four.py ADDED-AT READ-FILE SECOND-LOOK-FILE < note-with-section-11 > candidate
READ-FILE: the sentence, in italics under the heading, on how this section was read.
SECOND-LOOK-FILE: the clause on the referee's look at the author's changed text."""
import sys
s = sys.stdin.read(); when = sys.argv[1]; read = open(sys.argv[2]).read().strip(); look = open(sys.argv[3]).read().strip()
sec = f"""
## 12. Added {when}: the lemma on signs for every odd n and every multiset

*{read}*

Section 11 bounds the multiset. This section needs no bound. **For every odd n and every nonempty multiset on Z_n the lemma on signs holds**: each element can be given a sign so that the signed mask is divisible by no Φ_d, d | n. It is Theorem 4 of a fourth fresh instance (`lean-lift/four/sign-four-result.md`), which I asked for with the three earlier results, their referee's reports and the note of section 11 in hand. More exactly: of the 2^m ways of signing m elements, counted with multiplicity, more than 0.1549 are good if m is odd, more than 0.039 if m is even and at least 14, and at least 1/2, 3/8, 3/16, 9/128, 11/256, 23/1024 for m = 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12.

**How.** Contract the multiset as in section 11 until no level can be contracted: then every level of every tree has a group with all its p subclasses occupied, and a signing that is good for the contracted multiset is good for the first. Now let each element take its sign by a fair coin, independently of the others. Let d > 1 divide the order of the contracted group, and let p^i be the power of a prime p in d. By the slice criterion (Lemma 1 of the note of section 11), Φ_d divides the signed mask only if in every group at level i of the p-tree the p subclasses have congruent slices. For a group with k occupied subclasses that happens for at most the share 2^(−min(k, p − 1)) of the signs of its own elements, and different groups have no element in common. Because the levels below i have full groups, at least 1 + (i − 1)(p − 1) groups at level i hold an element, and one of them is full; so the share of signings for which Φ_d divides the mask is at most 2^(−i(p−1)). With the best prime of d it is at most 2^(−μ(d)), where μ(d) is the largest v_p(d)·(p − 1) over the primes p of d. The number of odd d with μ(d) ≤ t is ∏_p (1 + ⌊t/(p − 1)⌋), far smaller than 2^t, and the sum of 2^(−μ(d)) over all odd d > 1 is a constant, W = 0.84447…, less than 1 whatever n and the multiset are. With an odd number of elements Φ_1 never divides the mask (the sum of the signs is odd), so some signing is good. With an even number m, Φ_1 divides the mask for the share C(m, m/2)/2^m of the signings. For m ≤ 12 only the d with μ(d) ≤ m − 1 can occur, and the six sums are below 1 as they stand (the largest, at m = 12, is 1001/1024). From m = 14 on the level d = 3 is counted more exactly (at most 5/32 in place of 1/4), and the sum is below 0.961.

**Whose it is.** The count is the fourth instance's: one prime charged for each level, the constant W, the case of an even number of elements, and the theorem. It stands on the contraction of section 11, which is mine, and on the slice criterion of the same note; and its bound for one prime, 2^(−i(p−1)), is the bound of the third instance's Lemma T, carried over from sign vectors on a tree to independent signs on the elements. Two leads of my brief to it, given there as unchecked thoughts, are in it: to give each element its own independent sign, a level then costing a power of 1/2 that grows with the number of occupied classes; and that a sum over the levels, weighted by how many elements they need, might converge. The head of its text says what it took from each, in words that its referee asked for and its author agreed to (`lean-lift/four/sign-four-revision-1.md` has the author's own account). Theorem 7, the recursion Γ and the descent modulo q of sections 9 and 10 are not used, so the result does not depend on which n those cover.

**In print.** A fresh instance with the web looked for the statement in four forms (signed sums of roots of unity that vanish at no n-th root; polynomials with coefficients −1, 0, 1 and a prescribed support; units of the rational group ring of a cyclic group; signed circulant matrices): 31 searches, 26 queries of Stack Exchange's own search, and then the lists of the works that cite the two nearest papers. It did not find the statement, nor the contraction used for signs. That is what those searches found, and not proof that the statement is new (`lean-lift/four/scout-four-report.md`, `scout-four-report-2.md`). The nearest thing it found is M. W. Meckes, "Some results on random circulant matrices" (2009; arXiv:0902.2472), Theorem 6: a circulant matrix of odd order n whose n entries are independent random signs is singular with probability at most the sum of 2^(−φ(m)) over the divisors m > 1 of n, by the same union bound over the cyclotomic factors. There every coefficient is a sign. With a prescribed support one factor can divide the mask for half of all signings, and several factors at once (two elements that are congruent modulo several d), so that the union bound does not close as it stands; the contraction is what removes those levels. The search also met a preprint of 2026 on integer tilings that slices a tile by its lowest base-p digit (C. Li, J. Wu, W. Hu, C. Wang, E. Wang and F. Zhu, arXiv:2609.14026): that reduction is about a tile and its complement, and has no signs in it. Of the papers the search names I have read Meckes's theorem with the first part of its proof, and of the preprint the abstract, the outline of the method and the statement of its Theorem 3.3; nothing else.

**How it was checked.** A fresh instance refereed the result (`lean-lift/four/referee-four-report.md`; its programs and outputs are in `lean-lift/four/referee-four/`). It read the whole proof and, with the new use in mind, Lemma 1, Lemma 2 and §3 of the note of section 11, and found no error in the mathematics and no wrong number. It recomputed the constants with programs of its own (its bound is W < 0.8444737843; for every even m up to 400 the sums, with the level 3 counted as in the proof, are below 1). It counted the good signings exactly, with every signing, for 3,099,711 multisets on 17 groups from Z_9 to Z_315 (every multiset that contains 0, up to a number of elements that depends on the group), for every multiset of 14, 16, …, 24 elements on Z_9 (22,096,846 of them), and for others, among them 111 built so that the bound for one level is attained; no bound was violated. It required two changes, neither of them to the mathematics: the fact that after contracting every level has a full group is a sentence of §3 of the note and not part of its Proposition 3, where the text had sent the reader; and the account of what the proof took from the leads of my brief. It made four suggestions. The author made all six changes, and the same correction in five more places. {look} A program of mine gives the same W and the same sums for an even number of elements (`lean-lift/four/four-mine/w_check.py`). Before the instance started I had sealed my odds for what it would bring: 5 in 100 for this outcome (`lean-lift/four/expect-sign-four.md`; scored in `score-sign-four.md`). Nothing of this section is in Lean, and no person has read it.

**What it changes above.** The lemma on signs holds for every odd n; sections 9, 10 and 11 give other proofs for the cases they cover. Lifting along 2 (section 7) needs the lemma on signs only for the odd part of N, so now: for every N, every nonempty multiset on Z_N is the reduction of a multiset on Z_2N whose mask has no cyclotomic divisor Φ_d with d dividing 2N and not N; and for every set P of divisors of N the sizes of the nonempty multisets with exactly the pattern P are the same on Z_N and on Z_2N. The lemma on lifts for an odd prime r holds for every N already (section 7). So on paper the same is true with Z_{{N·s}} in place of Z_2N, for every N and every s ≥ 1. In Lean the lift along an odd prime is proved for every N, and the lift along 2 only for the N whose odd part is p^a·q^b (section 7).
"""
a = "\n## Files\n"
assert s.count(a) == 1
s = s.replace(a, sec + a)
k = s.index("· section 11: `lean-lift/small/`"); e = s.find(" · ", k + 5); e = e if e > 0 else s.find("\n", k)
entry = (" · section 12: `lean-lift/four/` (the result `sign-four-result.md` as revised after its referee's report and `sign-four-result.as-refereed.md` as the referee first read it, with the list of changes; "
         "the instance's report and its two replies to the referee; my brief to it and the brief to its referee; the referee's reports with its programs and outputs in `referee-four/`; the instance's programs and outputs in `general3/`; "
         "my own check of the constants and of a plan for Lean in `four-mine/`; my sealed sheet `expect-sign-four.md` and its scoring; the brief and the two reports of the search in print, with the search's scripts and without the pages it fetched)")
s = s[:e] + entry + s[e:]
sys.stdout.write(s)
